          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-06d71e5250469d71\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Prince Vasili directly avows that he loves the princess and the other women like his own children. The appeal “you know that” presents the affection as an already established truth. The utterance makes no substantial commitment or relational undertaking, and nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge rather than a calculated assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The sincerity and strategic purpose of the avowal are doubtful, but deception or manipulation would not alter its predominantly truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Prince Vasili directly tells Katerina Semyonovna that he loves her and the other women like his own children.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal occurs during Prince Vasili’s carefully managed discussion of Count Bezukhov’s impending death, will, and heirs. It reassures or flatters Katerina immediately before he pivots to his own family’s financial interest and seeks information about the will and legitimating letter. This strongly suggests strategic, possibly insincere use, while the core utterance remains an assertion of affection rather than a commitment or expressive reflex.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Я вас всех люблю, как своих детей, ты это знаешь” (“I love you all as my own children; you know that”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense avowal and appeal to the addressee’s prior knowledge strongly support truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Prince Vasili introduces the statement by saying that one must think about the future and about “all of you.”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal is part of a deliberate persuasive argument, not presented as an involuntary affective discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward he says that he must also think of his own family and turns to the inheritance, the will, and Pierre’s possible legitimation.\",\"supports\":\"The placement indicates strategic reassurance or manipulation, while supplying no independent performative undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration depicts Prince Vasili as proceeding “not without an inner struggle,” with nervous facial movements and frightened glances.\",\"supports\":\"His delivery is controlled but uneasy; emotional strain alone does not establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.93}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately captures the core event as a strategically situated avowal of affection. Its questionable sincerity and manipulative role belong to contextual interpretation, not to a missing core-force category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
